Revision history for App-WebPerimeterScan

0.001.0	Thu Nov  5 08:04:59 PM EST 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Scope file is the authorisation boundary; out-of-scope hosts are never
	  contacted, including hosts discovered by following links
	- Non-blocking TCP connect scan with a short banner read
	- TLS certificate, chain verification, protocol-version and cipher checks
	- Security header, cookie, CORS, HTTP method and mixed-content checks
	- Well-known sensitive path probes, filtered against a non-existent path
	  so applications that answer 200 for everything do not produce noise
	- PHP2Shell (CVE-2026-109618/109619) version check for disclosed PHP
	  versions
	- text, JSON and HTML reports; --fail-on controls the exit status
	- Source-aware pass: --docroot resolves each finding's URL to the real
	  file under the document root and --source maps it to the route that
	  serves it, so a sensitive file present in a docroot is reported even
	  when the server did not serve it
	- Report the endpoints the source defines that the crawl never reached
	- Record the response header block behind the header findings in each
	  host entry of the report, parsed and raw, so a finished scan can be
	  audited without repeating the requests; the page, the plaintext hop and
	  the CORS and OPTIONS probes are all kept

	- Every message, warning and error comes from one catalogue
	  (App::WebPerimeterScan::Messages) with placeholders, sprintf formats
	  and plural and select forms, chosen by LANGUAGE, LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES
	  or LANG; English is the only language so far
	- --format now has the short form -f, as documented
	- --fail-on, --format, --ports, --docroot and --source are validated
	  before any traffic is sent, not after the scan
	- A host the scanner refused or could not finish is reported as a
	  high-severity scan-error, so --fail-on high exits non-zero
	- Declare MIN_PERL_VERSION 5.014, which Sub::Private requires
	- Require Net::SSLeay 1.92, which compiles against OpenSSL 3 where 1.46
	  cannot, and lower the IO::Select and IO::Socket::INET floors to 1.17
	  and 1.31, the versions the suite is actually tested against

	[ Bug Fixes ]
	- Classify private addresses correctly: 192.0.0.0/8 and 223.0.0.0/8 were
	  treated as private, while multicast, TEST-NET, benchmarking and IPv6
	  private ranges were not; every address a name resolves to is checked,
	  not only the first
	- Refuse private targets reached by following links through a wildcard
	  scope entry, which bypassed --allow-private
	- Run the HTTP-to-HTTPS check for scope entries given as https:// URLs
	- Detect TLS 1.0 and 1.1 on OpenSSL 3, whose default security level
	  refused the probe handshakes so legacy protocols were never reported
	- Report a server that speaks only legacy TLS instead of skipping it
	- Inspect TLS on 8443 when that is the only TLS port open, not on 443
	- Accept a 307 redirect to HTTPS
	- csp-wildcard no longer fires on host wildcards such as
	  https://*.example.com; csp-unsafe judges only script-src and
	  default-src and honours nonces, hashes and strict-dynamic
	- Mixed content: link rel=canonical and similar are not resources,
	  and a stylesheet is active, not passive, content
	- An HTTP connection failure no longer reports the TLS error of an
	  earlier connection
	- Keep the value of a header with white space before its colon
	- Escape the generated time and version in the HTML report
	- Send the real release in the User-Agent, not 0.01
	- Count robots.txt Disallow lines and unvisited routes correctly; a
	  slice past the end of the list padded the count
	- Index only route definitions whose pattern starts with / in --source
	- Correct the clean target in Makefile.PL, which named the wrong
	  directory
	- Confirm the connection in the serial port scan instead of trusting
	  IO::Socket::INET, which returns a socket whose connect is still in
	  progress; on macOS every refused port was reported open
	- Fall back to connecting one port at a time where fcntl(F_SETFL) does
	  not exist; Windows has no such flags and the scan died on the
	  undefined F_GETFL macro instead of returning a result
	- Report the certificate subject and issuer when IO::Socket::SSL hands
	  them back as plain strings; both names came out empty, compared equal,
	  and every certificate was reported as self-signed
	- Read the certificate expiry through Net::SSLeay when IO::Socket::SSL
	  does not offer not_after from peer_certificate(), so expired and
	  soon-to-expire certificates are reported instead of skipped
